{
  "typedTransactionAndAccessListAcrossUpgrades": {
    "_info": {
      "comment": "The typed-transaction envelope and what an access list does. Every payload runs the same body -- the gas of one EXTCODESIZE against a fixed address -- so any difference is the transaction carrying it, not the code. Index 0 is a legacy transaction and the control: accepted at every upgrade, which separates the envelope being refused from the body being wrong. Index 1 is a typed transaction with an EMPTY access list; index 2 is the same envelope listing the address the body touches. Through Phoenix both typed payloads are refused and carry an expected exception instead of a result, so nothing about the list can matter yet. From Magneto all three are accepted and the typed pair separates: an empty list leaves the address cold at 2,607 gas, listing it makes the first touch warm at 107. Index 1 is what makes that attributable -- it shares the envelope with index 2 and the cost with index 0, so acceptance and pre-warming are measured separately rather than one inferred from the other. The control also moves at Magneto, 707 to 2,607, which is the cold-access repricing landing in the same upgrade and asserted on its own elsewhere.",
      "oracle": "core-geth production, evm t8n at each upgrade's rules, chain id 61",
      "oracle-version": "core-geth modernized 1.13.0-26975d6a. NOT v1.12.21, which this field previously named: production v1.12.21's t8n fork table has no ETC_DieHard, ETC_Gotham or ETC_DefuseDifficultyBomb, so it cannot address three of the twelve labels below and cannot be the sole generator. The modernized build's fork list is production's plus exactly those three.",
      "generated": "2026-08-23",
      "signing": "txbytes carries the signed transaction; the signing key is a filling input, not a fixture field, and the harness reads `sender`.",
      "control": "Index 0 is a legacy transaction, accepted at every upgrade. Index 1 shares index 2's envelope but carries no entries.",
      "rejection-label": "Two names, bar-separated, because two vocabularies are in use and no single name is mapped by both. TransactionException.TYPE_NOT_SUPPORTED is the current spec-tests enum member -- \"Transaction type is not supported on this chain configuration\" -- and TransactionException.TYPE_1_TX_PRE_FORK is what the primary consumer maps onto its own refusal. A reader takes the intersection of the names stated here with the refusals it can produce, so naming both satisfies either. CORRECTED 2026-08-24: this entry previously stated the single name TR_TypeNotSupported and said it was taken from the corpus. It was not. The corpus label is TR_TypeNotSupportedBlob, blob-specific and used 24 times in one archived file; TR_TypeNotSupported with the suffix dropped appears in no corpus and in no client, so it mapped to nothing and every case carrying it diverged instead of passing -- an expectation that could not be met, which reads exactly like a rule the client got wrong.",
      "unfilled-typed-before-die-hard": "ETC_Frontier, ETC_Homestead and ETC_GasReprice carry an expectation for index 0 only. A fixture must carry the signed transaction, and under those upgrades' rules a typed envelope cannot be signed at all -- the signer predates replay protection and handles only the legacy form -- so there is no encoding to record. That is a limit of what this format can express, not a claim that a typed transaction would be valid there; at consensus it would fail to decode as a transaction. The rejection IS asserted from Die Hard onward, where the transaction can be built and is then refused.",
      "unfilled-no-fork-name": "No expectation for ETC_Thanos: the generator's fork table has no name for them, so their rules cannot be selected. A tooling gap, not a claim that the case does not apply.",
      "unfilled-not-a-state-rule": "No expectation for ETC_DAOFork, ETC_DAOWars, ETC_FrontierThawing, ETC_MESSOff, ETC_MESSOn: these change no state-transition rule, so a state fixture has nothing to assert about them. Covering them needs block-level or chain-selection tests.",
      "verification": "2026-08-25: every expectation in this file was re-run through core-geth's OWN statetest runner (evm statetest) at the build named in oracle-version. This is a second-opinion check the suite had never had -- the roots here were previously only ever produced, never re-verified. Corpus-wide result: 531 of 546 subtests pass. The 15 that do not are every expectException case in the suite and they are NOT failures of these roots: geth's statetest builds its executed message from the `transaction` object's `sender` field and never validates a signature or an envelope, so it structurally cannot express 'this transaction is refused at this fork'. See FIXTURE-FORMAT.md, 'What the reference runner cannot check'."
    },
